---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import WidgetWrapper from '~/components/ui/WidgetWrapper.astro';

// Visual left, prose right — the fourth beat, closing the alternation.
//
// **`Operation` is the operative term across every surface; the props are what differ.**
// `<Operation operation="order.place" subject={order}>` gates a control in the design system,
// `dna({ address, operation })` stamps one on an element in a host application, and this is
// the same concept bound to a tool call. An earlier draft called this `governed()`, which
// named what we do to the thing rather than the thing — and the platform names things
// (Action, Anchor, Key, Lens, Operation), because the whole argument is that an operation is
// the unit and the surfaces are just where you meet it. Prose still says "act" where that
// reads better, which is what the Overlay's own walkthrough does.
//
// The code IS the visual here, and it is the most intuitive one available: what a reader is
// really asking — how much of my agent do I have to change — is answered by the line count
// before they have read a word of it. The tool body stays as a comment, because the comment
// is the claim.
//
// One block, and it used to be two. The second showed `guard.decide` for a call site the
// wrapper does not fit — a leftover from when this section framed the install as "declare,
// then enforce". Once `Operation` takes `perform` the wrapper *is* the enforcement, so a
// second snippet taught a second API on a panel whose whole claim is that there is one. It
// also leaned on a `subjects` value from nowhere, and explaining where that comes from means
// opening actor resolution, which this section deliberately does not. The escape hatch is
// real and it is in design.md; it is documentation, not a reason to make the answer longer.
//
// The trust properties are a list rather than eight cards. Each is a property @dna/guard
// asserts about itself; the four kept are the ones a buyer raises unprompted, and the rest
// are FAQ answers.

const declare = `import { Operation } from "@dna/agent-ops";

export const approveInvoice = Operation("invoice.approve", {
  target: (args) => args.invoiceId,
  perform: async (args) => { /* your code, untouched */ },
});`;

const trust = [
  {
    icon: 'tabler:bolt',
    lead: 'No network call at the moment of decision.',
    body: 'The rules are a local snapshot, not a fetcher — it could not call out even in principle. Your latency is unchanged, and your agent does not stop when we do.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'tabler:clock-exclamation',
    lead: 'Staleness never permits.',
    body: 'Reported on every verdict and bounded by a limit you set. Refusing on it would make a slow refresh your outage; permitting on it would make the bound an attack.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'tabler:arrows-split',
    lead: 'Drawing fails open, binding fails closed.',
    body: 'What may be offered leaves your agent as it was. What may happen refuses. A thing that permits when it cannot decide authorizes nothing.',
  },
];
---

<WidgetWrapper id="install" containerClass="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-10 md:gap-14 items-center">
    <!-- ── The picture: the whole installation, at a glance ─────────── -->
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900 overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
      <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/8 flex items-center gap-2">
        <Icon name="tabler:file-code" class="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-primary flex-shrink-0" />
        <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">The whole installation</span>
        <span class="ml-auto text-[10px] font-mono text-accent">5 lines</span>
      </div>

      <pre class="p-4 text-[11px] leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto text-slate-300"><code set:text={declare} /></pre>

      <div class="px-4 py-3 border-t border-white/8 bg-slate-900/60">
        <p class="text-[10px] text-slate-500 leading-snug">
          That is everything. The wrapper resolves the target and refuses before <code class="text-accent">perform</code
          > runs, the tool body is not touched, no framework is replaced, and a tool that names no operation is left alone
          rather than reported as broken.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── The prose ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div>
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary mb-4">Install and trust</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-5">
        One wrapper,<br class="hidden sm:block" /> and nothing else changes.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Two questions, and they are really one: what does this cost me to try, and what does it do to the thing I am
        already running in production. Each answer below is a property the enforcement package asserts about itself, not
        a promise we are making on its behalf.
      </p>

      <ul class="space-y-3.5 mb-7">
        {
          trust.map((t) => (
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <Icon name={t.icon} class="w-4 h-4 text-primary flex-shrink-0 mt-1" />
              <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-white">{t.lead}</strong> <span class="text-muted">{t.body}</span>
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-primary/30 bg-primary/5 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">Or install nothing at all.</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Claude, Cursor, your own loop — any MCP client reaches the same model through the same tools, with no
          integration to build. And <strong class="text-white">asking is free</strong>: an agent that can only read is
          an inspector, and inspectors are unlimited on every plan, forever.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</WidgetWrapper>
